{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A=0 \\\\ 3\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{27\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186524966 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A=0 \\\\ 3\\pi B=\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{27\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186524967 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1?\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186524967 1071 "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186524980 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,\n$$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186524980 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must contain ${x}^{3}$ and ${x}^{7},$ respectively. Since there is no ${x}^{7}$ term in $P$ that could possibly cancel the ${x}^{7}$ term in $Q,$ the leading term in $P+Q$ still contains ${x}^{7}.$ Therefore, the degree of $P+Q$ must always be $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{7}.$ Then, $\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot P}{Q}=\\frac{2{x}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{3}}{{x}^{7}}=2x,$ which is a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186524992 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},\n$$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186524992 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{4}+2.$ Then $\\frac{{x}^{4}}{P(x)}=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+2},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\n- Statement II is true. The highest-degree term in $P$ must contain ${x}^{4}.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ must contain ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{4}={x}^{4+4}={x}^{8},$ meaning that ${x}^{4}\\cdot P(x)$ has degree $8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=-{x}^{4}.$ Then ${x}^{4}+P(x)={x}^{4}-{x}^{4}=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $4.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186524999 1071 "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186524999 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186525014 1071 "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186525014 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186525064 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186525064 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186525986 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int [{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x=\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186525986 1071 "$\\int [{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)-1]\\text{d}x=$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186525988 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,5]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[5,7]$\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},\n$$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186525988 1071 "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,5]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[5,7]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186525988 1074 "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}\n$$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[1,5]$ and $[5,7]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =5-1=4, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =7-5=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=