ative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2})\n$$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\sqrt{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{3(3)}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{9}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-4).$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186527965 1074 "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\