-1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0+0 \\\\ 0+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186532691 1071 "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186532691 1071 "Starting with the standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion for $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186532692 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186532692 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186532692 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186532692 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-8 \\\\ -4+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186532693 1074 "We write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $ϕ\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/df/dfc7359ad44caa959459676a1e54bf1575e3c7e0f771e92a748