y}{x}\\,dy+dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the helix parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186534979 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ converge or diverge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186534979 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186534979 1074 "Suppose we have the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186534979 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}}\\right)}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186534981 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy+z\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]?$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186534981 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int